00:14:42.000 --> 00:15:25.000
which they thought would be the least controversial of the Asian identities. But through all of this, nothing about the character actually changed. All this Orientalism, this frustrated Bruce. I mean, they're trying to get me to do too many things that are really for the sake of being exalted. He said as much in an interview he did with the Canadian broadcaster, Pierre Breton. And I mean, it's always that pigtail and bouncing around chop chop, you know, with the Iceland and all that. I have already made up my mind that in the universe. United States, I think something about the Oriental, I mean, the true Oriental should be shown.
他们认为这会是亚裔各种身份中最少引发争议的一个。但经历这一切后，角色的内核其实一点也没变。所有这些东方主义，让李小龙感到十分沮丧。我是说，他们试图让我去做太多纯粹为了炫耀而存在的事情。他在接受加拿大广播员皮埃尔·布雷顿采访时曾这样说过。我的意思是，总离不开那根辫子，还有蹦蹦跳跳的“嚓嚓”声，你知道的，还有冰岛之类的。我已经在心里做好了打算，在美国的土地上，我认为应该展现出有关东方人的真实一面。

33
00:15:25.000 --> 00:16:15.000
Oriental, a word that was so universally used at the time, even Bruce was calling himself that. But meanwhile, across the country, there are kids who felt frustrated in the exact same way. Japanese, Chinese, Filipino American college students were all fed up with being lumped together by white folks in power. But unlike Bruce, they were going to organize. We're now moving into the late 1960s, and across the country, tensions are rising. And what we see is this growing anti-war movement, right? People rising up against the Vietnam War. A lot of the anti-war movement was happening on college campuses like Berkeley,
“东方人”（Oriental），这个词在当时被普遍使用，甚至连李小龙自己也这么称呼。但与此同时，在全国各地，有些年轻人感到了完全相同的沮丧。日裔、华裔、菲律宾裔的美国大学生们，都受够了被当权白人粗暴地归为一类。但与李小龙不同的是，他们打算组织起来。时间来到了20世纪60年代末，全国各地的紧张局势不断加剧。而我们看到的是日益高涨的反战运动，对吧？人们纷纷站出来抗议越南战争。许多反战运动都发生在大学校园里，比如伯克利，

34
00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:40.000
where two American-born Asian students, Uji, Yiyoka, and M.O.G, had been organizing. But by the spring of 1968, they'd started to notice something. For one, there were more and more Asian folks, people who were people who were, who looked like them showing up at these demonstrations. And the more they looked around, the more disconnected they felt from the main message of the movement at the time. Young people are out on the street saying, bring the boys home.
两个美籍亚裔学生——乌吉·吉冈（Uji Yiyoka）和M.O.G.一直在那里参与组织活动。但到了1968年春天，他们开始注意到一些现象。首先，越来越多的亚裔，也就是那些长得跟他们一样的人，开始出现在这些示威游行中。然而他们环顾四周越多，就越觉得与当时运动的核心诉求产生了一种疏离感。年轻人们走上街头高喊：让小伙子们回家。

35
00:16:40.000 --> 00:17:02.000
And they are looking at this, and they're saying, that doesn't seem to be what the movement should be about. We're seeing people who look like our parents just getting bombed. Their home places are being torn up. If you think about it, like, that's the reason we're here. It's not just about bringing the boys home. It's about ending American imperialism.
而他们看着这一切，心里却在想：运动的重点似乎不该只是这样。我们看到那些长得像我们父母的人正在遭受狂轰滥炸。他们的家园被彻底摧毁。如果你仔细想想，这才是我们来到这里的真正原因。这不仅仅是关于让小伙子们回家。这是为了终结美国的帝国主义。

36
00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:49.000
Then they had this idea. They were organizers for this new political party, the Peace and Freedom Party, which had formed around an anti-war agenda. So they decided to look over their own party's voter registration lists to identify any Asian people who might have felt the same way. So they take their lists, they look at all the names of folks on that list, and they go, hmm, you know, that person. person's last name is Kim, that person's last name is Chang, like, that person's last name is Wong, and they're like, we should call all of them and invite them to this meeting. They bring this group of folks together, and it's, you know, it's not many folks. Six people or so show up, and they are having this discussion about how to address the war. And the conversation quickly takes
接着，他们想到了一个主意。他们是一个新政党“和平与自由党”（Peace and Freedom Party）的组织者，该党是围绕反战议程组建的。于是，他们决定翻阅自己党派的选民登记名册，找出可能也有同感的人。于是他们拿着名单，看着上面所有人的名字，然后心想：嗯，你知道的，那个人姓金（Kim），那个人姓张（Chang），就像那样，那个人姓黄（Wong），然后他们觉得：我们应该给所有人打电话，邀请他们来开个会。他们把这群人聚到了一起，你知道的，人数其实不多。大约六个人出席了，他们开始讨论如何应对这场战争。而当大家开始倾诉内心的真实感受时，谈话迅速朝着

37
