00:22:46.000 --> 00:23:23.000
So these Hong Kong action movies, they're not just huge in Hong Kong. I mean, they're huge, huge in Hong Kong. But they make it over here. Tell me about where they thrive, how they sort of infiltrate culture here in the U.S. This is a moment where Hollywood is collapsing. You know, the old studio system that's been going strong for over half a century is showing a lot of signs of cracking. The movies Hollywood had poured millions into were flopping at the box office. The older generation was at home watching their color TVs, and the kids didn't want to watch another sound of music.
所以这些香港动作片，它们不仅在香港大火。我的意思是，它们在香港简直火得一塌糊涂。但它们也传到了我们这边。跟我讲讲它们是在哪里大受欢迎的，它们是如何渗透进美国本土文化的。这是一个好莱坞正在崩塌的时期。你知道的，那个已经强盛了半个多世纪的旧制片厂体制正显现出大量的崩溃迹象。好莱坞砸下数百万美元制作的电影在票房上惨遭滑铁卢。老一辈人在家里看彩色电视，而孩子们才不想看什么《音乐之声》。

50
00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:50.000
Hello Dolly. And so what's the breath of fresh air that comes along? Well, 1971, Melvin Van Peebles independently releases this movie called Sweet, Sweet Back's badass song. And it just is a sensation. The movie follows Sweetback, a black performer at a brothel who fights back against racist police officers, and it completely shattered the way old Hollywood worked. It makes over a million dollars off a tiny, teeny, tiny budget.
还有《甜心先生》。那么，随之而来的清流是什么呢？1971年，梅尔文·范·皮布尔斯（Melvin Van Peebles）独立发行了一部名为《甜蜜的电影》（Sweet Sweetback's Baadasssss Song）的电影。它引发了巨大的轰动。影片讲述了皮布尔斯——一个在妓院工作的黑人表演者，奋起反抗种族主义警察的故事，它彻底颠覆了旧好莱坞的运作方式。它用微乎其微的超低预算，赚取了超过一百万美元的票房。

51
00:23:50.000 --> 00:24:27.000
And it makes that money just. by playing in the inner cities, like in the scariest parts of America. The movie was released to gritty grindhouse theaters that played everything the mainstream rejected on a 24-hour loop. They just play Blacksportation movies. They play foreign films. They play porn movies, right? Sweet Sweet Sweetback is a classic of the Blacksploitation genre, a new form that was exploding at the time, where Black protagonists took down white authority with violence, style, and glamour. So kung fu movies come in on the heels of that.
而且这些钱全靠在内城上映赚来的，比如美国治安最可怕的那些地方。这部电影被投放到粗粝的“剥削电影院”（grindhouse theaters）里，这些影院全天候不间断地播放着主流社会所唾弃的一切内容。它们只播放黑人剥削电影。它们播放外国电影。它们播放色情电影，对吧？《甜蜜的电影》是黑人剥削电影（blaxploitation）类型的经典之作，这是一种在当时蓬勃发展的新兴类型片，片中的黑人主角以暴力、风格和魅力击垮了白人权威。所以功夫电影正是顺应了这股潮流。

52
00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:47.000
Kids are coming in and they're watching these black exploitation movies. They're there for an afternoon. It's super hot. And so they're just going to watch all the movies that are playing in there. Shaft maybe comes on like and then the Chinese boxer comes on. And they're like, what is this? But it's dubbed. You know, the dubs are kind of corny and a little hilarious.
孩子们走了进来，观看了这些黑人剥削电影。他们在那里待上整整一个下午。天气超级热。所以他们干脆把里面正在播放的所有电影都看了一遍。也许接着放映的是《夏福特》（Shaft），然后《中国拳王》（The Chinese Boxer）又上场了。他们心想：这是什么？但它是配音的。你知道的，配音有点老土，甚至还有点滑稽。

53
00:24:47.000 --> 00:25:15.000
And at the same time, they're kicking butt, you know? And the plot lines are pretty much the same. These are folks who are on the margins. They've been harmed. They've been disenfranchised. And they're avenging their folks against these tyrannical and corrupt bosses and politicians. So they stick around and they realize, oh, wow, these are great. In 1972, Fist of Fury opens in New York's Chinatown Pagoda Theater.
与此同时，他们在片中大显身手，对吧？而且剧情套路基本上都差不多。这些都是处于社会边缘的人。他们受到了伤害，被剥夺了权利。他们正在向那些暴虐且腐败的老板和政客复仇，为自己人讨回公道。于是他们留下来观看，并意识到：哦，天哪，这些电影太棒了。1972年，《精武门》在纽约唐人街的宝塔剧院（Pagoda Theater）上映。

54
00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:57.000
And for the first time in the theater's history, half of the crowd was non-Chinese. And it's this movie where Bruce Lee gains a whole new movie. audience, a deeper layer to his fame. When Bruce first came out in the mid-60s as Cato, he wasn't a hero for the Asian-American radicals. He was just like, oh, that's another sort of guy who's going to serve his white master. But when Fist of Fury plays, a lot of those folks who were calling themselves Asian-American, they're sitting there in the theater and they're watching Bruce Lee kicking down that sign that says no dogs in Chinese aloud. And they're seeing black folks, white folks, Latino folks,
在该影院历史上破天荒地，有一半的观众都是非华裔。正是在这部电影中，李小龙收获了全新的一批观众，他的名气达到了更深的层次。当李小龙在60年代中期以“加藤”（Cato）的形象初次登场时，他并不是亚裔美国激进分子心目中的英雄。人们只会觉得：哦，那又是一个准备服侍他白人主子的家伙。但当《精武门》上映时，许多自称为亚裔美国人的人坐在影院里，看着李小龙一脚踢碎那块写着“华人与狗不得入内”的牌子，他们看到黑人、白人、拉丁裔人，

55
00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:22.000
you know, American Indian folks, they're all going crazy just like they are. And so they're realizing, oh, this is a moment and this is a thing. This guy is an underdog. Like, he's fighting on our side. He's exactly what we want to see represented and to be about. By 1973, the Kung Fu movie craze is at an all-time peak. In that year alone, six Kung Fu flicks out of Hong Kong hit number one
你知道的，还有美洲印第安人，他们全都像他们自己一样陷入了疯狂。于是他们意识到：哦，这是一个历史时刻，这是一件大事。这家伙是个弱者。而且，他在为我们这一边战斗。他完全就是我们渴望看到的、所代表的那种形象。到了1973年，功夫电影热潮达到了史无前例的巅峰。仅在那一年，就有六部来自香港的功夫电影冲上了票房榜首，

56
00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:50.000
the box office, and 15 more hit in the top 50 as they played in Chinatowns and segregated neighborhoods across America. Which movie encapsulates the peak of this era? I mean, to me, I think it's probably Enter the Dragon, but maybe you have a different opinion about this. No, I think Enter the Dragon is absolutely it, yeah. And the reason it does is because, you know, it comes in this year, 1973, where we're kind of at peak third world culture, if you're looking at it that way.
另有15部挤进了前50名，它们在美国各地的唐人街和种族隔离社区中上映。哪部电影最能概括这个时代的巅峰？我的意思是，对我来说，我觉得可能是《龙争虎斗》，但你对此可能有不同的看法。不，我觉得《龙争虎斗》绝对是当之无愧的。之所以如此，是因为你知道的，它正是在1973年上映的，如果我们从这个角度来看的话，当时我们正处于第三世界文化的巅峰期。

57
00:26:50.000 --> 00:27:31.000
When Jeff says third world culture, he's referring to a movement where black and Asian activists, among other groups, started seeing their struggles as part of something bigger, not just a fight for rights at home, but a connection to people pushing back against power everywhere in Vietnam and Algeria and the Congo. The idea was that a so-called ghetto in Oakland had more in common with a foreign village under a colonial rule than anyone in power wanted to admit. And that feeling was everywhere in culture. One of the top albums in 1973 was called The World is a Ghetto. Bob Marley was gaining popularity everywhere. Hip-hop was literally invented that year.
当杰夫提到“第三世界文化”时，他指的是这样一场运动：黑人和亚裔激进分子以及其他群体开始将他们的抗争视为更大宏图的一部分，这不仅仅是为了争取国内权利的斗争，而是与世界各地反抗强权的民众联系在一起——从越南、阿尔及利亚到刚果。当时的观点是，奥克兰所谓的“贫民窟”与一个受殖民统治的外国村庄之间，有着比当权者愿意承认的更多的共同点。而这种感觉充斥在整个文化中。1973年最热门的专辑之一就叫《世界是个贫民窟》（The World is a Ghetto）。鲍勃·马利（Bob Marley）到处都在获得人气。嘻哈音乐正是在那一年被发明的。

58
00:27:31.000 --> 00:28:14.000
We're at sort of this peak moment where folks are just looking for a different kind of hero. And that's Bruce. That's what Bruce Lee is. In Enter the Dragon, Bruce Lee teams up with Jim Kelly, a black martial artist, and John Saxon, a white martial artist, to form a multicultural fighting squad. It really is just taking everything and showing a kind of commonality, a common struggle. And, you know, it's funny because it didn't, it wasn't really planned this way. So, for instance, they chose three actors as leads because they were actually unsure that Bruce Lee was going to be able to carry a movie all by himself, right?
我们正处于这样一个巅峰时刻，人们只是在寻找一种不同类型的英雄。那就是李小龙。这就是李小龙代表的意义。在《龙争虎斗》中，李小龙与黑人武术家吉姆·凯利（Jim Kelly）和白人武术家约翰·萨克森（John Saxon）联手，组成了一个多元文化的战斗小队。这其实就是将一切融合在一起，展现出一种共通性，一种共同的抗争。而且，你知道的，这很有趣，因为这一切并不是事先计划好的。例如，他们选择三位演员担任主角，其实是因为他们不确定李小龙能否独自撑起一部电影，对吧？

59
00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:45.000
So let's put a black man in there, of course, a nod to black deportation. And of course, let's put a white man in there. Whatever they intended, the movie spoke directly to the third world movement. There's an iconic scene where the black member of the trio, Jim Kelly, is riding through a harbor in Hong Kong, looking around at people living on these crammed houseboats. They don't live so big over there. Gettos are the same all over the world. They stink.
所以让我们在里面安排一个黑人，当然，这是对黑人剥削电影的一种致敬。当然，让我们再安排一个白人。无论他们的初衷如何，这部电影直接呼应了第三世界运动。有一个标志性的场景，三人组中的黑人成员吉姆·凯利骑马穿过香港的港口，环顾四周那些住在拥挤船屋里的人们。“他们在那里住得可不算宽敞。全世界的贫民窟都一样。它们都臭气熏天。”

60
00:28:45.000 --> 00:29:17.000
The ghettos, all over the world, they're all the same. That's one of those lines that it's just like, What could be more third world than that particular line? And it's one of those lines that just sings down the generations. And of course, there's the incredible scene with Bruce Lee and his nunchucks, where a shirtless Bruce surrounded again on all sides takes the nunchucks from one of the men and fends off three different attackers. When Bruce is in these scenes with his nunchucks, people are just magnetized, right?
“全世界的贫民窟，它们全都是一样的。”这是那种听了之后会让人感叹的台词之一：还有什么台词能比这更具“第三世界”色彩呢？这是那些代代相传、余音绕梁的台词之一。当然，还有李小龙和他的双节棍的精彩场景，光着膀子的李小龙再次被四面合围，他从其中一人手中夺过双节棍，击退了三个不同的袭击者。当李小龙出现在这些挥舞双节棍的镜头中时，人们简直被深深吸引了，对吧？

61
00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:42.000
And then the natural reaction is you're going to go out and you're going to make your own. you're going to go and buy some, you're going to try to do it yourself. And so suddenly there's all these young people, like mostly black folks and yellow folks and brown folks, just in the streets with nunchakus. In a way, the nunchucks are the perfect symbol for this moment. Here's this weapon, not born from any single nation,
而自然的反应就是你会走出去，自己也去做一个，你会去买一些，你会试着亲自去耍一耍。于是突然之间，所有这些年轻人，主要是黑人、黄种人和棕色人种，手里都拿着双节棍走上了街头。某种程度上，双节棍正是这个时代的完美象征。这件武器并非诞生于某一个单一的国家，

62
00:29:42.000 --> 00:30:26.000
but a mashup of fighting traditions across Asia, one that is now being embraced by a coalition of minorities in America. It's scrappy. It's the kind of thing you pull out when you're surrounded on all sides, when you're backed into a corner. And this scares the hell out of people who are already feeling threatened by black exploitation movies and kung fu movies. They just don't want to see this image of, you know, young folks of color with weapons. Immediately after Enter the Dragon was released, officials started referring to the people who used nunchucks as muggers and members of street gangs. Mainstream outlets like the New York Times ran articles saying that they were turning up in the hands of youth across America.
而是亚洲各地武术传统的融合，如今它正被美国的一众少数族裔联盟所拥抱。它透着一股野性。这是当你四面楚歌、被逼入绝境时才会掏出来的东西。而这把那些本来就因为黑人剥削电影和功夫电影而感到受到威胁的人吓得半死。他们就是不想看到这种有色人种年轻人手持武器的形象。就在《龙争虎斗》上映后不久，官员们开始把使用双节棍的人称为“持强抢劫犯”和“街头帮派成员”。《纽约时报》等主流媒体甚至发文称，双节棍正在落入全美各地青少年的手中。

63
00:30:26.000 --> 00:31:01.000
Newsweek called them killer sticks. And the following year, five states banned them entirely. Unlike laws for pocket knives or baseball bats, where police usually have to prove you intend to use them as a weapon, nunchucks were classified alongside brass knuckles and sought-off shotguns. Simply having a pair sitting in your bedroom could mean a felony charge. I was looking at the number of nunchakus that were sold on New York. And it was something, it came up to like maybe less than 3,000 a year. There was like 15 million guns that are sold a year, right?
《新闻周刊》称它们为杀人棍。第二年，有五个州完全禁止了双节棍。与对袖珍刀具或棒球棍的法律不同，警察通常必须证明你打算将它们用作武器，而双节棍则与指节铜套和短管霰弹枪归为一类。仅仅在卧室里放一副双节棍就可能意味着重罪指控。我当时在查纽约出售的双节棍数量。结果大概是每年不到3,000副。每年大约卖出1500万支枪，对吧？

64
00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:40.000
So that tells you that it wasn't about the nunchakus. It was about the folks who were holding the nunchukus. And even more, it's about what that meant to folks who were seeing those folks with the nunchakus. It wasn't until 2018 that these states finally began to repeal their bans. But even as the government tried to ban nunchucks, they couldn't stop them from influencing culture. The kung fu movies sort of somewhat ride, you know, in the wave of the black exploitation movies. But then the kung fu movies sort of feed back into black culture as well. Can you talk about the adoption of the sort of kung fu aesthetic and things like that in black and hip-hop culture?
所以这告诉你，问题不在于双节棍本身。而在于那些拿着双节棍的人。更重要的是，这对于那些看到这些人拿着双节棍的人来说意味着什么。直到2018年，这些州才终于开始废除禁令。但即使政府试图禁止双节棍，也无法阻止它们对文化产生影响。功夫电影有点像是在搭乘黑人剥削电影的顺风车。但随后功夫电影又反过来滋养了黑人文化。你能谈谈黑人和嘻哈文化中对这种功夫美学及类似事物的接纳吗？

65
00:31:40.000 --> 00:32:32.000
Yeah. It's like young black and brown kids in the inner cities who are discovering these kung fu movies and they're going crazy. you know, and it continues, right? It continues all through the 80s and the 90s. And in 1993, right, like 20 years after Enter the Dragon, we get Wu-Tang Clan, you know? Bruce Lee made a huge impact. He and his nunchucks represented a huge change in the way that the world saw Asian Americans and how they saw themselves, which is pretty remarkable, given how short his life actually was. Bruce Lee died in 1973 when he was only 32 years old, at the peak of his career, only a few months after Enter the Dragon was released.
是的。就像内城区的年轻黑人和棕色人种孩子发现了这些功夫电影，然后他们为之疯狂。你知道的，而且这种影响一直在持续，对吧？它在整个80年代和90年代一直延续着。而在1993年，也就是《龙争虎斗》上映20年后的好时候，我们有了武当帮，你知道吗？李小龙产生了巨大的影响。他和他的双节棍代表了世界看待亚裔美国人以及他们看待自己方式的巨大转变，考虑到他短暂的一生，这相当了不起。李小龙于1973年去世，年仅32岁，正值他事业的巅峰，就在《龙争虎斗》上映几个月后。

66
00:32:32.000 --> 00:33:41.000
The official cause of death was an allergic reaction to equiogesic, a prescription painkiller that he'd taken earlier that evening to treat a headache, and he died from swelling to the brain. I wonder if you thought about what would have happened to Bruce Lee if he hadn't died so young. I do. I do wonder about that. I think about it actually a lot. I think about it in the context, too, in terms of representation. And I think about how it took almost 50 years for us to get to a place where you could have Michelle Yo receive an Academy Award in a role in which she could represent all these different ways that we appear. So not just sort of a kick-ass kung fu master, but she could be a bedraggled housewife and laundromat owner. And she could play literally dozens of different types of roles and represent the broad range of what Asian Americans and Pacific Islanders are. And it hurts me that it's taken that long for us to be able to kind of get to that particular point.
官方死因是对一种名为equiogesic的处方止痛药产生过敏反应，他那天早些时候曾服用该药来治疗头痛，他因脑部肿胀而死。我想知道你是否想过，如果李小龙没有那么年轻就去世，他会怎么样？我会的。我确实想过这个问题。其实我经常这样想。我在代表性的语境下也会考虑到这一点。我想象着，我们花了将近50年的时间才走到今天这一步，让杨紫琼能够凭借一个角色荣获奥斯卡奖，在这个角色中，她可以展现我们呈现自己的所有不同方式。不仅是那种厉害的功夫大师，她还可以是一个衣衫褴褛的家庭主妇和自助洗衣店老板。她可以扮演字面意义上几十种不同类型的角色，代表亚裔美国人和太平洋岛民的广阔范围。一想到我们花了这么长时间才走到这个特定的点，我就感到心痛。

67
00:33:41.000 --> 00:34:23.000
I like to think that had Bruce lived, that he might have been able to move away from just being a martial artist and that he could have been able to explore all of these different types of things. that represented the breath of his humanity. It kind of makes me a little melancholy thinking about that. It's devastating to imagine this, this alternate reality where Bruce Lee doesn't die at 32, where he keeps working and keeps shifting culture at the same time. Perhaps there would have been a time sooner or later where he could have set the nunchucks down,
我喜欢这样想：如果李小龙还活着，他可能已经摆脱了仅仅作为一名武术家的限制，能够去探索所有这些代表他人性广度的不同事物。想到这里，我甚至有点伤感。想象这样一个平行时空是令人心碎的：在这个时空里，李小龙在32岁时没有去世，他继续工作，同时继续改变着文化。也许在不久的将来，总有一天他可以放下双节棍，

